schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Hot Wallets vs. Cold Storage
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    Contrasts internet-connected "hot" wallets (fast, convenient, higher theft
    risk) with fully offline "cold storage" (hardware or paper, slower to
    access, much lower theft risk), and recommends a layered custody model —
    hot for operational funds, hardware cold storage for reserves/grants,
    multi-signature for organizational decisions.
  audience: >-
    community organizers or treasurers deciding where to keep organizational
    or personal crypto holdings
  known_tensions:
    - convenience-of-hot-wallets-vs-security-of-cold-storage
    - upfront-hardware-wallet-cost-vs-perceived-affordability-for-small-holdings
  related_concepts:
    - layered-custody-hot-cold-multisig-by-purpose
  related_resources:
    - cryptosteel-metal-seed-phrase-storage
    - cryptocurrency-security-standards-ccss
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/hot-vs-cold.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: the article's "Try This" self-audit checklist
    (identify holdings, classify hot/cold, check seed-phrase storage, rate
    exposure 1-10) was excluded as a standalone object — it is an exercise
    prompt, not a typed knowledge object, and is preserved in full in the
    source file. MetaMask, Ledger, Trezor, and Safe/Gnosis Safe were not
    re-created as new resource objects: equivalent resources ("MetaMask",
    "Hardware / cold wallets (Ledger, Trezor)", "Safe (Wallet)") already exist
    in data/kb/resource.yaml from a prior ingestion slice.
